          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong doctrine
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jon+Beck"} Jon Beck
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorification
            of the concept of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"}> theory
            ”. Thus, a doctrine could also reasonably be called a “2-theory.”
          %p
            Here we are using the word “theory” in a vague non-precise sense. For instance, there is a theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} categories with finite product
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig categories
          %p
            Moreover, just as categories are objects of a 2-category (the categorification of a category), ordinary
            %em theories
            are objects of some
            = succeed "." do
              %em doctrine
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            can be defined in any such category. Likewise, the theory of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
          %p
            Moreover, in general there is a “free” category containing a model (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"}> walking model
            ”) of some given theory: for instance there is a category with finite products freely generated by a group object (namely, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            of a group), and a monoidal category freely generated by a monoid object (namely, the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/augmented+simplex+category"} augmented simplex category
            %em models
            are categories in some doctrine can itself be regarded as a category in that doctrine, with the property that the category of models of a theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is the hom-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(T,K)
            in the relevant doctrine. In fact, we can fruitfully think of a doctrine
            %em as
            the collection of all theories in that doctrine, since (for example) any category with finite products can be regarded as a “theory” that can be interpreted in any other such category, via finite-product-preserving functors.
          %p
            For this to make sense, whatever a doctrine is, it must in particular induce a 2-category of its models. Or, at least, of its models in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-theory"} 3-theory
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#as_2monads As 2-monads
          %p
            A number of people, starting with Lawvere, have defined “doctrine” to mean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
            (particularly a 2-monad on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %p
            At a lower level, the “theories” considered above of which a doctrine is supposed to be a categorification are also
            %em not
            the same as
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
          %p Likewise, not all “morphisms of doctrines” are representable by morphisms of 2-monads.
          %h3#as_2categories As 2-categories
          %p
            Another possible definition of “doctrine” is just “a
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+doctrines"} Higher doctrines
            (given a notion of higher category).
          %p However, while useful, this approach has the disadvantage of being too broad: there are many 2-categories that we would not really want to consider as being the category of models of some doctrine.
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-algebraic+theory"} 2-algebraic theory
          %h3#as_cartesian_double_theories As cartesian double theories
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LambPatt23"} Lambert &amp; Patterson, 2023
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+category"} cartesian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            and a model of a cartesian double theory to be a finite product-preserving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} lax functor
            out of it.
          %h3#as_logics As logics
          %p
            The word “doctrine” can also be used to design a particular setting for categorical logic (e.g., categories with finite products,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric logic
            etc…; see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
          %h3#as_a_class_of_limits As a class of limits
          %p
            Especially in the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+categories"} accessible categories
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutativity+of+limits+and+colimits"} commutativity of limits and colimits
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weights
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sound+doctrine"} sound doctrine
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-algebraic+theory"} (∞,1)-algebraic theory
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-algebraic+theory"} 2-algebraic theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                \/
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
                %strong doctrine
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-theory"} 3-theory
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-monad"} (∞,1)-monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} (∞,1)-operad
          %h2#references References
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
            ,
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.acsu.buffalo.edu/~wlawvere/Beck%20posting.htm"} 23 Mar 2006
          %blockquote
            %p
              The word “doctrine” itself is entirely due to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jon+Beck"} Jon Beck
              and signifies something which is like a theory, except appropriate to be interpreted in the category of categories, rather than, for example, in the category of sets; of course, an important example of a doctrine is a 2-monad, and among 2-monads there are key examples whose category of “algebras” is actually a category of theories in the set-interpretable sense. Among such “theories of theories”, there is a special kind whose study I proposed in that paper. This kind has come to be known as “Kock-Zoeberlein” doctrine in honor of those who first worked out some of the basic properties and ramifications, but the recognition of its probable importance had emerged from those discussions with Jon.
          %p The concept of doctrine is apparently first appears in print in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere69
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Ordinal sums and equational doctrines
          %p General discussions of the “doctrine” concept:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/06/algebraic_geometry_for_categor.html"} Algebraic Geometry for Category Theorists (blog post)
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/johnbaez/show/Doctrines"} Doctrines by Dolan and Baez
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/volumes/10/20/10-20abs.html"} A duality relative to a limit doctrine
                by C. Centazzo and E.M. Vitale
          %p
            Doctrines are treated as cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            in:
          %ul
            %li#LambPatt23
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Michael Lambert
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Michael+Lambert"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Evan+Patterson"} Evan Patterson
              %em Cartesian double theories: A double-categorical framework for categorical doctrines
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05384"}> arXiv:2310.05384
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            A pedadogically aimed study of higher doctrines, using the simple definition of doctrines as (weak)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            described by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Categorical Toolbox
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards the mathematics of quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-04564-1_2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-04564-1_2
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+doctrines"}> Higher doctrines
                \.
